# Visualizing Data {#sec-data-viz}

:::{.callout-note}
#### Packages Used
This chapter uses the following packages: [ggplot2](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org), [sjPlot](https://strengejacke.github.io/sjPlot/), [gtsummary](https://www.danieldsjoberg.com/gtsummary/), [waffle](https://github.com/hrbrmstr/waffle), [ggpubr](https://rpkgs.datanovia.com/ggpubr/),
[ggdist](https://mjskay.github.io/ggdist/index.html),
[ggridges](https://wilkelab.org/ggridges/), [scales](https://scales.r-lib.org), [gridExtra](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/gridExtra/index.html), 
[likert](https://github.com/jbryer/likert) for the `pisaitems` data,
[RColorBrewer](); [kableExtra]()
and the [penguins](https://allisonhorst.github.io/palmerpenguins/index.html) data. 
:::


::: {.cell}

:::



Visualizing your data is hands down the most important thing you can learn to do. There are links to additional resources at the end of this document for additional learning. 

## Choosing your audience


The level of detail you put into your graphs/tables will depend on who
the graph/table is for. In general, you will be visualizing data for
three audience types: yourself and/or your data management team, an internal audience (other specialties within your organization, research coordinators), and external, professional audiences. The tabs below describe the three audience types and show an example graph for each.

**Who is your audience?**

::: panel-tabset
### Yourself

Graphs/tables for yourself and/or your working team can be "quick and dirty."
These graphs are for getting a quick look at the data and are meant for
people who are already familiar with the data.


::: {.cell fig-weight='3'}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Show the code"}
plot_frq(pen$island)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-2-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


### Internal Audience

When presenting for an audience with some familiarity with the project and the data analysis process, your graphs don't have to be completely perfect, but they should be fairly polished and be understandable. A good rule of thumb for these graphs is if they
were to end up being published as-is without your knowledge, you
wouldn't be completely embarrassed to see them in print.


::: {.cell fig-weight='3'}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Show the code"}
plot_frq(pen$island, title = "Count of Penguins by Island") +
        xlab("Island") +
        theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-3-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


### Professional

Professional quality graphs and tables have the highest amount of detail
and take the most amount of time to make. They should be able to be
interpreted by people not familiar with the project or data analysis, even
without reading the rest of the report.


::: {.cell fig-weight='3'}

```{.r .cell-code  code-fold="true" code-summary="Show the code"}
plot_frq(pen$island, title = "Count of Penguins by Island") +
          labs(subtitle = "Includes penguins of Adelie, Gentoo, and Chinstrap species") +
          xlab("Island") +
          ylab("Penguin Count") + 
          theme_minimal()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-4-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::

:::


## The Data

We will use the `penguins` dataset that comes with the `palmerpenguins` package. This dataset contains size measurements for three penguin species observed on three islands in the Palmer Archipelago, Antarctica. Review `?penguins` to learn about the variables we will be using. 



::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
pen <- palmerpenguins::penguins
```
:::


::: {.callout-warning appearance=simple}
The following sections show examples of data visualizations by variable type (categorical, continuous, or both) and number of variables. These visualizations have been created in base R and at least one other R package to demonstrate different ways of creating similar visualizations and demonstrate key package features.

:::

## One Categorical Variable

Both Nominal and Ordinal data types can be visualized using the same methods: tables, barcharts and pie charts. 


### Tables

Frequency and proportion (relative frequency) tables are the most common way to get summary statistics of a categorical variable.

::: {.panel-tabset}

## base

The `table()` function produces a frequency table, where each entry represents the number of records in the data set holding the corresponding labeled value. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
table(pen$species)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```

   Adelie Chinstrap    Gentoo 
      152        68       124 
```


:::
:::

There are 152 Adelie penguins, 68 Chinstrap penguins, and 124 Gentoo penguins in this dataset. 


## gtsummary

:::{.callout-warning appearance=simple}
The [tbl_summary](https://www.danieldsjoberg.com/gtsummary/reference/tbl_summary.html) function in the [gtsummary](https://www.danieldsjoberg.com/gtsummary/) package can create prettier tables more easily than in base R. The [tbl_summary](https://www.danieldsjoberg.com/gtsummary/reference/tbl_summary.html) function defaults to creating a summary table of the entire data set, so you must select only the variables you want to summarize before running the function. 
:::


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
pen |> select(species) |> tbl_summary()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}

