### Other

::: {.callout-warning appearance=simple}

Although tables and barcharts are popular data visualizations for a reason, you may want to show your data in a different way. Below are some examples of alternate data visualizations that show similar information to tables/barcharts. 

:::  


::: {.panel-tabset}
#### Cleveland Dot Plot

Another way to visualize categorical data that takes up less ink than bars is a Cleveland dot plot. Here again we are plotting summary data instead of the raw data. This uses the `geom_segment` that draws the lines from x=0 to the value of the proportion (named `Freq` because of the way `data.frame` works).


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(spec.props, aes(x=Freq, y=Var1)) +  
  geom_point(size = 3) + xlab("Proportion of penguins") + 
  theme_bw() + ylab("Species") +
  geom_segment(aes(x=0, xend=Freq, y=Var1, yend=Var1), color='grey50')
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-14-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


#### Pie Chart

Just like `barplot()`, `pie()` takes a table object as its argument. 

**base**

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ps <- table(pen$species)
pie(ps)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-15-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


Pie charts are my _least_ favorite plotting type. Human eyeballs can't distinguish between angles as well as we can with heights. A mandatory piece needed to make the wedges readable is to add the percentages of each wedge. 

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
pie(ps, labels = paste0(names(ps), ' (', prop.table(ps)*100, "%)"))
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-16-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


**ggplot**

And here I thought pie charts couldn't get worse... I'm not a fan at all of the ggplot version. So I'm not even going to show it. [STHDA](http://www.sthda.com/english/wiki/ggplot2-pie-chart-quick-start-guide-r-software-and-data-visualization) has a great tutorial that does show you how to make one. 

However -- Never say never. [Storytelling with data](http://www.storytellingwithdata.com/blog/2019/8/8/forty-five-pie-charts-never-say-never) has a blog post with an example of a *good* use of pie charts. 


#### Waffle Chart 
This type of chart is not natively found in the `ggplot2` package, but its own `waffle` package. These are great for infographics. 



