## Faceting / paneling 

This is a good place to introduce a term called `faceting`. The definition is _a particular aspect or feature of something_, or _one side of something many-sided, especially of a cut gem_. Basically instead of plotting the grouped graphics on the same plotting area, we let each group have its own plot, or facet.  

We add a `facet_wrap()` and specify that we want to panel on the species group. Note the twiddle in front of species. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(pen, aes(x=bill_depth_mm, fill=species)) + geom_density() + facet_wrap(~species)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-85-1.png){width=864}
:::
:::


The grid placement can be semi-controlled by using the `ncol` argument in the `facet_wrap()` statement. 

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(pen, aes(x=bill_depth_mm, fill=species)) + 
  geom_density() + facet_wrap(~species, ncol=4)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-86-1.png){width=864}
:::
:::


It is important to compare distributions across groups on the same scale, and our eyes can compare items vertically better than horizontally. So let's force `ncol=1`. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(pen, aes(x=bill_depth_mm, fill=species)) + 
  geom_density() + facet_wrap(~species, ncol=1)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-87-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


### Paneling on two variables

Who says we're stuck with only faceting on one variable? A variant on `facet_wrap` is `facet_grid`. Here we can specify multiple variables to panel on. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(pen, aes(x=body_mass_g, fill=sex)) + geom_density() + facet_grid(sex~island)
```

