### Consistent Themes

It is considered good practice to create graphs with as little "visual
noise" - extra lines, unnecessary colors/grayscale, numbers with too
many significant figures, etc. - as possible. Visual noise can be
overwhelming and cause key points to be missed because it forces your
audience to process extra information.

The easiest way to minimize unnecessary visual noise is to set a global
theme for your graphs after loading in your R packages. 

Which graph is easier to interpret from a quick glance?


::: {.cell source-line-numbers='12'}

```{.r .cell-code}
this <- plot_grpfrq(var.cnt = pen$species, var.grp = pen$island, title = "This?") +
          labs(subtitle = "Count of Penguin Species by Island (2007-2009)") +
          xlab("Penguin Species") + ylab("Count") +
          set_theme(legend.pos = "top")

that <- plot_grpfrq(var.cnt = pen$species, var.grp = pen$island, title = "Or That?") +
          labs(subtitle = "Count of Penguin Species by Island (2007-2009)") +
          xlab("Penguin Species") + ylab("Count") +
          set_theme(legend.pos = "top",
            base = theme_minimal())

grid.arrange(this, that, ncol = 2)
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-105-1.png){width=1344}
:::
:::


An even better option is to use the following code chunk in the setup header to set a consistent theme across all of the graphs in your document. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
# For ggplot
ggplot2::theme_set(theme_minimal())

# For sjPlot
sjPlot::set_theme(base = theme_minimal())
```
:::


More ggthemes can be found
[here](https://yutannihilation.github.io/allYourFigureAreBelongToUs/ggthemes/). Remember that it is considered good practice to choose the theme with the least visual noise that still
conveys all the needed information for your graph.

## Troubleshooting 

### Problem: Missing data showing up as a category in ggplot? 

::: {.cell}

:::


Get rid of that far right bar!

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(NCbirths, aes(x=marital)) + geom_bar()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-108-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


::: {.callout-tip appearance=minimal collapse=true}

## Solution:
Use `dplyr` to select only the variables you are going to plot, then pipe in the `na.omit()` at the end. It will create a temporary data frame (e.g) `plot.data` that you then provide to `ggplot()`.


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
plot.data <- NCbirths %>% select(marital) %>% na.omit()
ggplot(plot.data, aes(x=marital)) + geom_bar()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-109-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


:::

### Problem: Got numerical binary 0/1 data but want to plot it as categorical? 

Consider a continuous variable for the number of characters in an email `num_char`, and a 0/1 binary variable `spam`. 

::: {.callout-tip appearance=minimal collapse=true}

## Solution:
Create a second variable `var_factor` for plotting and keep the binary `var` as 0/1 for analysis. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
email$spam_cat <- factor(email$spam, labels=c("Ham", "Spam"))
ggplot(email, aes(y=num_char, x=spam_cat)) + geom_boxplot()
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-110-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


:::


### Problem: You want to change the legend title for a `fill` or `color` scale.  

::: {.callout-tip appearance=minimal collapse=true}

## Solution:
Add the `name=` argument to whatever layer you added that created the legend. Here I specified a `fill`, and it was a `discrete` variable. So I use the `scale_fill_discrete()` layer. 


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(email, aes(y=num_char, x=spam_cat, fill=spam_cat)) + geom_boxplot() + 
  scale_fill_discrete(name="Ya like Spam?")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-111-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


Here, I `col`ored the points by a discrete variable, so the layer is `scale_color_discrete()`.

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
ggplot(email, aes(x=num_char, y=line_breaks, col=spam_cat)) + geom_point() +
  scale_color_discrete(name="Ya like Spam?")
```

::: {.cell-output-display}
![](data_viz_files/figure-html/unnamed-chunk-112-1.png){width=672}
:::
:::


:::


